      "title": "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "eb73671b-991b-5b90-a085-0b65b086d530",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/232b015a3c6bd1bf7d2eba3c085fba561810992b846b64ecde32519b8d9e1f15.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/d60aa065fa9d8b6af5e77329e4975ec13103a4804061a45acdbf933aeadf9ec5.png)",
